Changelog: 23-10-2026 15:22:20

Release 0.3.0.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 15:07:48

Added. Two boxes in the header after the firmware (owner, 23-10-2026): Reports, the input reports a second, and
Bluetooth off in, how long until the idle service switches a controller on Bluetooth off; the window is at least
1072 px wide for the six. For that countdown the service keeps when each controller was last used in
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bc250-usb-hdcontrol-idle.json (at most every 5 seconds; removed when it stops), so the window shows
the service's own time, not one of its own.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 14:44:00

Fixed. A controller on Bluetooth no longer reads "The update needs the USB cable" while it runs the latest firmware:
the firmware box and the Connection box's tooltip say so only when an update is out (owner, 23-10-2026: on the
latest firmware it confused).

Changelog: 23-10-2026 14:37:43

Added. With two or more controllers connected, a tab for each under the header instead of the list in it (owner,
23-10-2026): the player number its lights show, the model, the connection and the battery ("Player 2 · Edge ·
Bluetooth · 40 %"), all at a glance; a click shows that controller in the window. The idle service's notification
names the controller the same way ("DualSense Edge (player 2) switched off").

Changelog: 23-10-2026 14:11:39

Fixed. After a firmware update the window says to remove the controller from the system's Bluetooth settings and pair
it again: the update ends the pairing (owner's Edge, 23-10-2026: after 0x0200 to 0x0217 it blinked quickly and
switched off without reaching the board, and paired only after its old entry was removed). The wiki says so too.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 13:56:29

Added. Switch off when unused (owner, 23-10-2026: with a service, the PS5's choices, 10 minutes by default): a user
service that install.sh adds and --uninstall removes (bc250-usb-hdcontrol-idle, python -m usb_hdcontrol
--idle-watch, idle.py) reads the input reports of every controller on Bluetooth and, after 10, 30 or 60 minutes
without a button, a stick off its middle or a trigger pulled, ends its Bluetooth connection as Turn off does and
says so in a desktop notification (notify-send; also when it fails, owner 23-10-2026) and the journal, naming the
controller by the player number its lights showed ("DualSense Edge (player 2)"; each controller has its own wait); the
motion sensors do not count, controllers on the USB cable are left alone, and Never in the window turns it off. The
window's controller box has the choice, Switch Bluetooth off when unused; the service reads it again each time. Turn
off is now Turn Bluetooth off, as wide as Test vibration and the choice, the three spread down the column (owner,
23-10-2026). The drawing's d-pad and face buttons sit further towards the edges, each by 80 % of a face button's
size (owner, 23-10-2026).

Changelog: 23-10-2026 13:48:00

Added. The editor's Triggers box says that the range is saved in the profile, but on Linux the controller still
reports the whole travel (owner's Edge, 23-10-2026: R2 set to 0 to 50 % in Fn + Square, read back as 0 to 128, filled
like L2; Cross set to Circle did send Circle), and that a PS5 may use it (owner, 23-10-2026: keep it, with the note).

Changelog: 23-10-2026 13:38:36

Added. The drawing shows the player indicator under the touchpad, lit as the controller's own lights and large
enough to see on a big screen (owner, 23-10-2026: an 80-inch one; read from
hid-playstation's LEDs in /sys, which every user may read; only root may set them), and hovering over it names the
player: with two controllers connected, it tells which one the window shows (owner, 23-10-2026). The spec sheet no
longer lists it among the parts the drawing leaves out. The drawing's touchpad is wider; every other part moves
out by the same amount on its side and keeps its size (owner, 23-10-2026).

Changelog: 23-10-2026 13:33:06

Added. The profile card in use says Active and follows Fn + Triangle, Square, Cross or Circle live: the DualSense
Edge reports its active profile in byte 49 of its USB input report, upper four bits (read on the owner's Edge,
23-10-2026: 1 for Fn + Triangle, 2 for Fn + Square; over Bluetooth one byte further, not yet read). The same capture
showed a profile written by the app working on Linux: after Fn + Square, Cross set to send Circle sent Circle.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 12:53:43

Added. Create, edit and clear the DualSense Edge's profiles (owner, 23-10-2026). Under each card Create… on an empty
slot, Edit… and Clear on a profile, over the USB cable; the default profile (Fn + Triangle) is never changed, also
refused in profiles.py itself. The editor sets the name, each stick's curve and sensitivity (0 to 10), the triggers'
ranges and what each button sends. Each change saves a backup of the four profiles first, then writes three reports
with the slot's ID (0x60 Square, 0x61 Cross, 0x62 Circle) and each report's own CRC-32, or clears with 0x68, and reads
the slot back to check it. A new profile starts from the default profile's bytes and gets its own 16-byte ID; an
edited one keeps its own bytes; the bytes the app does not know stay as the controller made them. Tried on the owner's
Edge before (23-10-2026, a test script on the same reports): written, read back byte for byte, cleared, the default
profile unchanged; the stick curves' points follow the web application's tables. On the owner's Edge (23-10-2026)
the controller applied the button map and the curves itself on Linux: Cross set to Circle sent Circle, the left stick
on Digital jumped to the edge while the right one on Default moved smoothly.

