Release 0.2.0.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 08:16:14

Added. The drawing follows the controller live and does more (owner, 23-10-2026). It reads the input reports from the
hidraw node beside hid-playstation (inputs.py: report 0x01 over USB, 0x31 over Bluetooth): each button lights while
pressed, the Edge's Fn and back buttons too, the sticks move and the triggers fill. A Controller box holds it, with
the light bar's color (Blue, Red, Green, Purple, White, Off or any color; set as hid-playstation's output report
0x02 over USB, 0x31 with its CRC-32 over Bluetooth, again each time the controller connects while the app is open;
the drawing shows it) and Test vibration (the kernel's force feedback on the controller's input device: the left
motor, then the right, the drawing shaking on that side). During a firmware update the drawing shows data flowing
down the plugged-in cable and the touchpad filling. Redrawn: 85 % of its width at the same height, a larger d-pad and
face buttons, the face buttons' symbols in the first PlayStation pads' colors, a light bar along the touchpad, and
the gaming apps' blue (#0A55B0, #5B93DA on dark) for what is lit and the chosen profile instead of the theme's
accent. Read on the owner's Edge (23-10-2026, USB): the input report at rest parses as no button pressed, the hat at
8, the sticks within 3 % of the middle; the controller's own input device is the one without a suffix in its name
(event11), which the user may write to.
Then (owner, 23-10-2026, after every button and both motors worked on the owner's Edge): the d-pad's arms are white
with a black border and a gap between them; the turning with the mouse is gone (it did not look 3D); the drawing
stands on the left with the light bar's colors under it, and the light bar's color now, the hint and Test vibration
on its right; and the header has the battery as a phone shows it, upright, right of the boxes, instead of the Battery
box (battery.py: five modules from the bottom up, green, orange at 20 % or less, red at 10 % or less; while charging,
the modules above the level fill one after another, upwards). The firmware is one pill (owner, 23-10-2026: there were
two for it): the version the controller runs, "Current version", green when it is the latest; orange, "Update to" the
latest version, when an update can start, which a click starts; otherwise a click asks Sony's server again. It stays
clickable, so core's muted look of a disabled pill does not gray it while the controller is up to date, and the
window keeps the focus at start (a focused pill's blue border hid the orange). The battery is twice as wide, the same
height. The controller's top runs nearly straight out to small corners, so the bumpers and triggers sit on the body.
A controller plugged in while still connected over Bluetooth stays on Bluetooth (owner's board, 23-10-2026: hid-
playstation refused the USB device, "Duplicate device found for MAC address", and left its HID device without a
driver; the cable only charged it): the window says so (On Bluetooth, not the cable) and offers Use the cable, which
disconnects it with bluetoothctl disconnect (BlueZ 5.87 answers "Disconnection successful"; the first try took that
for a failure though the controller had let go); then Connect asks to plug the cable in again. The Edge's back
buttons are paddles behind the grips, sticking out from their inner edges, instead of dashed circles on the grips,
which did not read as buttons (owner, 23-10-2026). The battery stands as tall as the header, beside both rows, its
top level with the title's (owner, 23-10-2026). The Controller, Firmware and Profiles boxes have their title in a
blue bar along their top (pages.Section) instead of above their frame. Their borders are white at half strength on
the dark themes, so the 1 px line reads thin (owner, 23-10-2026); on Silver they keep the theme's edge, as white
would not show there. The firmware is two again (owner, 23-10-2026, now that the header has room): Installed
firmware, green or orange as before, and Latest firmware, the pill that turns orange and starts the update; every box
as wide as Bluetooth needs, so none changes width with the connection. The header's boxes and pills and the profile
cards have the sections' soft white edge (style.soften over core's surface style, in the app only). A neutral box,
pill or card has one even 1 px soft edge; a state's color keeps its 4 px left edge (owner, 23-10-2026). The window,
the introduction, the menu entry and the portal's summary no longer name the maker (owner, 23-10-2026): "the official
update server", "the official app for Windows", "the firmware's signature"; the wiki's privacy page and the license
note still do, as they must. The header's boxes are 5 % wider (owner, 23-10-2026: they looked cramped), and the
battery stands at the header's right end with its percentage inside, the number and "%" on two lines, centered. Every
button has a tooltip, the checkbox, the intro's Back and Next, the profile cards and the drawing too (owner,
23-10-2026; a test checks it). The Firmware box names the kept file of the latest version with its SHA-256 check, as
USB HD Cam does (owner, 23-10-2026): a green tick and "it is the known file" for a file the app knows, an orange !
for one it does not, with the hash; hashed once per file. The color buttons' and profile cards' tooltips each say
their own thing (they repeated one sentence): the color's name, the slot and its profile, or how to create one in an
empty slot. The drawing names the part under the mouse in its tooltip (owner, 23-10-2026: one text for the whole
drawing said the same everywhere): every button, the sticks, triggers, touchpad, light bar and USB port, the Edge's
Fn and back buttons, from shapes that match the drawn ones (drawing.regions). An i at the top left of the drawing
opens the spec sheet (owner, 23-10-2026; specs.py): what the controller reports now, never its address; the model's
facts with their official source, numbered (the Edge's from its instruction manual and the PlayStation Blog's launch
post: CFI-ZCP1, 26 January 2023, $199.99, 1,050 mAh, about 325 g; the price still listed on PlayStation's page, seen
by the owner); none yet for the plain DualSense, as no official source was found; and the parts the controller does
not report, moved out of the drawing: the stop sliders, the RELEASE latch, the reset button, the headset jack, the
charging contacts (which the manual does not describe; the owner found them), the microphone and the speaker. Turn
off ends a Bluetooth controller's connection, which switches it off; on the cable it stays on. The Firmware box reads
in blocks (owner, 23-10-2026): its three sentences one a line, a blank line, the controller's name in bold, its
firmware and build date (the target, hardware version and build are in the spec sheet), whether it is the latest, the
kept file's check and its SHA-256 on one line each, a blank line and the buttons; the text keeps the height of all
its lines (the SHA-256 line was cut off). The Controller and Firmware boxes are equally wide (owner, 23-10-2026), and
the light bar's circles run from the drawn controller's left edge to its right, spread out, with a color wheel for
any color in place of Other….

Changelog: 23-10-2026 07:43:46

Added. Pages after Sony's app for Windows (owner, 23-10-2026), with a drawing of the controller of the app's own
(drawing.py, QPainter in the theme's colors; no Sony artwork): Connect your controller while none is connected, a
USB-C cable going into the drawn controller's port again and again; an introduction of three pages after the first
controller connects (what the app does, the firmware update, the Edge's profiles or a DualSense's battery), each
lighting the parts it is about, once (Skip, and Show the introduction brings it back); and the main page with the
drawn controller beside the firmware box. The profiles are four cards side by side instead of a table; clicking one
lights on the drawing what it sets (the Fn buttons, and the sticks, triggers and buttons it changes from their
defaults). After an update the main page stays while the controller restarts, instead of showing Connect for those
seconds.

